.\" gzippy-format(5) — the legacy "GZ" FEXTRA subfield (pre-1.0 files).
.TH GZIPPY-FORMAT 5 2026-04-24 "gzippy 1.0.0" "File Formats"
.
.SH NAME
gzippy-format \- the legacy \(dqGZ\(dq FEXTRA subfield of pre-1.0 gzippy files
.
.SH STATUS
Retired as an OUTPUT format in gzippy 1.0.0: no compression level, thread
count, or flag makes gzippy 1.0 write it. The shipped binary emits plain
RFC 1952 gzip (one member at T1, concatenated members at T>1) with an empty
flags byte, and its output is interoperable with every compliant gzip tool.
.PP
The DECOMPRESSION side still reads the format for backward compatibility
with files produced by pre-1.0 releases, and this man page documents it for
those readers.
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
The legacy format is a convention layered on top of RFC 1952 gzip. A
pre-1.0 gzippy file is a sequence of one or more gzip members. Each parallel
block is one member, and each member carries an FEXTRA subfield that records
the total size of the member.
.PP
Compliant gzip readers ignore unknown FEXTRA subfields (RFC 1952 section
2.3.1.1) and decode the file normally. gzippy pre-1.0 used the subfield to
find block boundaries without inflating, which made parallel decompression
cheap on its own output. The marker was removed because enough third-party
gzip tools mishandle FEXTRA that the field made gzippy output
non-interoperable in the wild; the modern pipeline finds block boundaries
structurally instead, needing no in-band marker.
.
.SH LAYOUT (legacy)
Each member begins with the standard gzip header. The subfield sits inside
the FEXTRA area, which is selected by setting bit 2 (0x04) in the FLG byte.
.PP
.nf
    Offset  Size  Field         Value
    ------  ----  -----         -----
      0     2     ID1 ID2       0x1f 0x8b
      2     1     CM            0x08        (deflate)
      3     1     FLG           FEXTRA=0x04, optionally |FNAME|FCOMMENT
      4     4     MTIME         u32 LE, unix seconds
      8     1     XFL           0x00
      9     1     OS            0xff        (unknown)
     10     2     XLEN          0x08 0x00   (8 bytes of FEXTRA data)
     12     2     SI1 SI2       'G' 'Z'     subfield identifier
     14     2     SLEN          0x04 0x00   (4 bytes of subfield data)
     16     4     BLOCK_SIZE    u32 LE, total member size
     20     ...   FNAME?        NUL-terminated, if FNAME flag set
      ...   ...   FCOMMENT?     NUL-terminated, if FCOMMENT flag set
      ...   ...   deflate data
      ...   4     CRC32         u32 LE of uncompressed data
      ...   4     ISIZE         u32 LE, uncompressed size mod 2^32
.fi
.PP
BLOCK_SIZE is the length in bytes of the entire gzip member \(em from
the magic number through the ISIZE trailer. A reader that has just finished
member
.I M
can skip to byte
.RI offset( M )\ +\ BLOCK_SIZE
to find the next member, without looking at the deflate stream at all.
.
.SH VALIDITY
A legacy file is valid gzip iff every member is valid gzip. gzippy verifies
on decompression:
.IP \(bu 2
The magic number on every member.
.IP \(bu 2
The XLEN and SLEN framing around the "GZ" subfield when present.
.IP \(bu 2
CRC32 and ISIZE on every member.
.PP
If any check fails, gzippy aborts with a non-zero exit status and an error
written to stderr.
.
.SH COMPATIBILITY
Modern gzippy output (plain RFC 1952, no FEXTRA) decodes with every tested
tool: GNU gzip, Apple gzip, pigz, libdeflate, Go
.IR compress/gzip ,
Python
.IR gzip ,
Node.js
.IR zlib ,
and 7-Zip. Legacy pre-1.0 files additionally decode with tools that skip
FEXTRA per RFC 1952 (GNU gzip, pigz, Apple gzip, Python
.IR gzip ,
libdeflate); a reader that chokes on a pre-1.0 file is not honoring RFC
1952's FEXTRA rule.
.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gzippy (1),
.BR gzippy-tuning (7),
.BR gzip (1),
RFC 1952 (GZIP file format specification version 4.3).
.
